 "name": "Restorative Tourist Streetscapes – Scene Viewing, Ratings and SART",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Wang, Zhao, Xu, Li & Cheng (2026), \"Restorative streetscapes in high-density urban tourism: environmental archetypes, visual attention, and psychophysiological responses\", Frontiers in Public Health 14: 1981997. The six scenes are the paper's focal sites around Victoria Harbour, Hong Kong, one per environmental archetype from a PCA and K-means analysis of 66 tourism-related sites: Oil Street Art Space, Hong Kong Science Museum, Western Market, St. John's Cathedral, Old Wan Chai Post Office and Hong Kong Museum of Art (photographs from Figure 6, CC BY 4.0). Each scene is shown for 60 seconds in random order. Participants then rate being away, fascination, coherence/extent and compatibility (perceived restorativeness, mean of four, 1–7) and stress, calm, tension and positive affect (1–7), and complete an 8-event Sustained Attention to Response Task (7 go digits, 1 no-go digit 3); correct events and mean go reaction time are saved per scene. Sex, sleep and caffeine context are asked at the start. In the paper, Oil Street Art Space was rated most restorative (5.69/7) and Western Market least (4.17/7), while SART accuracy did not differ by scene. Differences from the original study: (1) no EEG, eye tracking, EDA or HRV; (2) the 120 s baseline, 90 s mild stress induction and 30 s break of each trial are left out (the paper does not describe the stressor); (3) the paper names the four restoration dimensions but not the item wording, so the item and affect wording is written for this template; (4) each participant sees all six scenes, whereas in the paper participants contributed one to four scenes; (5) the images are cropped from the published figure (scene letter removed) and are smaller than the originals.",
